# Line-ending normalisation -- LOAD-BEARING IN THIS REPO, NOT COSMETIC
#
# Why this file matters here:
#   The maintainer machine is Windows with `core.autocrlf=true`. Without
#   .gitattributes, every text file a contributor writes lands in git with CRLF
#   in the BLOB (autocrlf=true normalises on commit only for files git thinks
#   are text, and `git add --renormalize` never ran here), which then produces
#   three concrete failures in this specific project:
#     1. The Dockerfile / sh snippets and the browser JS get checked out with
#        CRLF on a Linux host (Railway/Render/Fly, GitHub Actions, the Muse VM).
#        A `#` comment line is harmless, but a CRLF inside a Dockerfile `RUN`
#        line continuation or a shebang breaks the build ("bad interpreter",
#        `$'\r': command not found`).
#     2. `skills/video-summary/references/*.md` and `prompts/*.md` are read
#        verbatim into LLM prompts, and `assets/browser-extract.js` is
#        eval'd in a browser page. CR characters change the bytes of a template
#        that is documented as "byte-identical to the desktop defaults", and a
#        CR can break JS ASI-sensitive lines.
#     3. CI diffs (`ruff format --diff`, pytest fixture comparisons against
#        files in tests/) fail on Windows contributors only -- the classic
#        "passes locally, red on CI" loop.
#   Normalising in git means the repository content is LF everywhere and
#   autocrlf can no longer make the blob depend on one person's git config.
#
# If you clone on Windows and prefer CRLF working copies, `core.autocrlf=true`
# keeps working as it does now; what stops varying is what gets *committed*.

# ---- default: normalise text, checkout LF (predictable on Linux hosts) ----
* text=auto eol=lf

# ---- source / docs that must stay LF ----
*.py text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.cfg text eol=lf
*.ini text eol=lf

Dockerfile text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
.railwayignore text eol=lf
.gitattributes text eol=lf
runtime.txt text eol=lf
LICENSE text eol=lf

# env templates must stay LF: they are fed to `docker compose --env-file`, and a
# CR there silently becomes part of a variable value.
deploy/env.example text eol=lf
*.example text eol=lf
*.env.example text eol=lf

# ---- binary: never touch bytes ----
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
*.model binary
*.bin binary
*.onnx binary
*.ct2 binary
*.gz binary
*.whl binary
*.tar binary
*.mp4 binary
*.m4a binary
*.mp3 binary
*.wav binary
*.ogg binary
*.opus binary
*.sqlite3 binary
*.db binary

# ---- diffs (cosmetic; helps review of the deploy files) ----
pyproject.toml text eol=lf diff=toml
*.yaml text eol=lf diff=yaml
*.yml text eol=lf diff=yaml
*.md text eol=lf diff=markdown
*.json text eol=lf diff=json
